extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Animation"
	- pagedescription = "How fast a Tablechart chart builds: element time, stagger, a maximum and easing, or no animation at all."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Animation
				p.lead How fast a chart builds.

	+section("Speed")
		p Each element takes #[code --tablechart-element-duration], the next one starts after #[code --tablechart-stagger-ratio] of that time, and if the whole chart would take longer than #[code --tablechart-max-duration], the starts move closer together.

		+style.
			.slow {
				--tablechart-element-duration: 0.9s;
				--tablechart-stagger-ratio: 0.5;
			}

		+chart
			figure.slow(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Variable
					th Sets
			tbody
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-element-duration
					td The time that one element (a bar, column or point) transitions: 0.5s by default.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-stagger-ratio
					td When the next element starts. It is a ratio of the #[code --tablechart-element-duration]: 0.24 of it by default. At 1, each element starts when the one before it is finished. At 0, all elements start together.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-max-duration
					td The longest a whole chart may take to build: four times the element time by default, 2s. It is a ceiling, never a target: a small chart is not slowed down. Above it, the starts move closer together; each element keeps its own time. Numbers fade in for at most a quarter of the maximum.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-circle-duration
					td How long a donut takes for a full circle: 1.5s by default. A slice takes its share of that.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-easing
					td The easing of bars, donuts, and lines and areas that grow upwards: #[code cubic-bezier(.26, .86, .44, .985)] by default. A line that builds from left to right does so at an even speed.
				tr
					td: code --tablechart-annotation-duration
					td How long an annotation line takes: 0.6s by default.

		p These are CSS variables, so they can be set on a chart, a section or a whole page. Set on a section, the maximum holds for each chart in it.

	+section("Waterfalls")
		p A waterfall builds as one motion at one speed: each column starts when the one before it ends, its connector draws meanwhile, and the last column slows to a stop. With steps, each step builds on its own. Under the maximum, the whole motion gets shorter.

		+chart
			figure.slow(data-chart="waterfall")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Picked', '74.5', 'total'], ['Windfalls', '28.2'], ['Bruised', '-16.1'], ['Peelings', '-13.3'], ['Pressed', '73.3', 'total']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Donuts")
		p A donut draws its ring in one sweep, with the same easing as a bar: fast at the start, settling at the end. A full circle takes #[code --tablechart-circle-duration], 1.5s by default, and each slice takes its own part of the sweep. With a stagger ratio below 1, the slices start sooner and overlap, and each slice eases on its own.



		+chart
			figure(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '42'], ['Jonagold', '28'], ['Braeburn', '18'], ['Gala', '12']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p A lower ratio starts the slices sooner, so they grow at the same time.
		+style.
			.together {
				--tablechart-stagger-ratio: 0.6;
			}

		+chart
			figure.together(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '42'], ['Jonagold', '28'], ['Braeburn', '18'], ['Gala', '12']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Old names")
		p #[code --tablechart-duration] and #[code --tablechart-stagger] still work until the next minor version, and the console says so. #[code --tablechart-duration] is now #[code --tablechart-element-duration]. #[code --tablechart-stagger] was a time, such as #[code 0.12s]; #[code --tablechart-stagger-ratio] is a share of the element time, such as #[code 0.24].

	+section("Lines and areas from below")
		p A line or area chart builds from left to right. Add #[code data-chart-animate="up"] to the figure to make it grow up from zero instead. The points rise with the line, and the numbers appear when it is done.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="area" data-chart-stack data-chart-shape="smooth" data-chart-animate="up" data-chart-legend)
				table
					thead
						tr
							th
							th North
							th South
					tbody
						each row in [['2020', '42', '18'], ['2021', '55', '26'], ['2022', '38', '31'], ['2023', '61', '24'], ['2024', '49', '35']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p To make every line and area grow up, set the #[code animate] option to #[code "up"]. Then #[code data-chart-animate="across"] makes one chart build from left to right again. Other chart types build as they always do.

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ animate: "up" });

	+section("No animation")
		p To show charts without animation, set #[code animate] to #[code false]. If the visitor prefers reduced motion, charts build without animation anyway.

		+codeblock('JS').
			init({ animate: false });
